('What was a donative?','A payment to soldiers linked to an emperor’s accession',['A tax exemption for every farmer','A title given to a junior emperor','A religious ban on coinage'],'Repeated accession payments could encourage troops to support repeated changes of ruler.'),
('Why does the lecture distinguish political turmoil from daily life?','Some institutions and activities continued despite instability at the top',['Political events never affect anyone','All provinces experienced identical collapse','Only emperors can leave historical evidence'],'The degree of social disruption must be investigated rather than inferred directly from a list of short reigns.'),
('What is coinage debasement?','Reducing precious-metal content while retaining a claimed face value',['Increasing metal content without changing value','Replacing every coin with a government bond','Prohibiting all exchange of goods'],'The lecture connects this fiscal expedient with inflation and uncertainty about value.'),
('In the lecture’s explanation, why might poorer coins remain in circulation?','People spend them while retaining better coins',['Every coin’s metal content is identical','The government forbids anyone to hold valuable objects','Better coins cannot be stored'],'Freedman uses the tendency to hoard better money to explain how poorer currency can dominate exchange.'),
('Why was information about land and population important to Diocletian?','It supported more systematic assessment and collection of taxes',['It replaced the need for an army','It established modern income-tax payroll records','It guaranteed equal wealth across the empire'],'Productive resources had to be assessed in an economy where wealth was not primarily represented by modern salaries.'),
('What was the tetrarchy’s basic structure?','Two senior emperors and two junior colleagues',['One emperor and three elected assemblies','Four unrelated independent republics','Two emperors without designated assistants'],'The Augusti and Caesars distributed responsibility, though cooperation and succession remained problematic.'),
('Which distinction best describes Diocletian’s reforming stance?','Conservative goals pursued through radical changes',['Revolutionary goals pursued without administrative change','An intention to abolish imperial government','A refusal to address frontier defense'],'He sought to preserve imperial order by significantly reorganizing its machinery.'),
('Why did the Edict on Prices struggle?','Fixed legal prices encouraged evasion and black markets',['It abolished all demand for goods','It permanently ended scarcity in every province','It applied only to imaginary commodities'],'Freedman explains how legal prices and actual market pressures could diverge.'),
